    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menkaure">Menkaure</a> of the Fourth Dynasty (2613–2494 BCE) the art of pyramid-building was perfected and the pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a> were raised.</li><li>The king, not yet called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, was held to be the incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, a living god who ruled absolutely and could demand the services and wealth of his subjects.</li><li>In the Sixth Dynasty power passed to hereditary nomarchs, and a drought between 2200 and 2150 BCE that stopped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood brought famine and the kingdom's collapse.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Old Kingdom shipbuilders used neither treenails nor metal fasteners: planks and superstructure were tightly tied and bound together with rope, and such ships sailed the length of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

