    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/djoser"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djoser.jpg" alt="Djoser" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djoser_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tomk2ski, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old Kingdom is the name Egyptologists give to the period from the Third to the Sixth Dynasty, 2686–2181 BCE, the first of three 'Kingdom' periods that mark the high points of civilisation in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. The break with the Early Dynastic Period is not dynastic: the last king of that age was related to the first two kings of the Old Kingdom, and the royal residence stayed at Ineb-Hedj, the Egyptian name for Memphis. What justifies the division is the revolutionary change in architecture and the effects that large-scale building had on Egyptian society and its economy.</p><p>The first king of the Old Kingdom was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a> of the Third Dynasty, who ruled some time between 2691 and 2625 BCE and ordered a pyramid, the Step Pyramid, in the necropolis of Memphis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a> is credited with the development of building in stone and with the conception of the step pyramid as a form, and a new era of building began. In this age the formerly independent states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a> under the king's rule, their former rulers forced to serve as governors or work in tax collection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> believed the king to be the incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, linking the human and spiritual worlds, and saw the universe as working in cycles that the king on earth laboured to keep stable. Information from the Fourth to the Sixth Dynasties is scarce, and the history of the era is literally 'written in stone', built from the monuments and their inscriptions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sahure"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahure.jpg" alt="Sahure" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sahure">Sahure</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Sahure_and_a_Nome_God_MET_DP-1691-04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old Kingdom's economy turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and on the king's command of labour and goods. The pyramids and their complexes were the largest works of the age; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> used more stones than any other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, and the canal to Lake Moeris, dug around 2300 BCE, was likely also the source of water to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a> pyramid complex centuries earlier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s growing appetite for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a> and frankincense, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and other useful metals drove the building of ships fit for the open sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> for cedar and sailed the length of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, for ebony, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and aromatic resins; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahure">Sahure</a> of the Fifth Dynasty commanded an expedition there. The shipwrights used no pegs or metal fasteners but bound planks and superstructure tightly together with rope. There was also direct trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><p>The rulers of the Fifth Dynasty sent expeditions to the stone quarries and gold mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and the mines of Sinai, and the Sixth Dynasty's peak under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-i">Pepi I</a> and Merenre I brought flourishing trade and several mining and quarrying expeditions. Art served religion and ideology rather than enjoyment, and its workshops produced sculpture in hard stones, gneiss, graywacke, schist and granite, whose colours carried meaning: black for the soil after the Nile flood, green for vegetation and rebirth, red for the sun, white for purity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pepi-ii.jpg" alt="Pepi II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii">Pepi II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_head_from_a_small_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old Kingdom's wars were campaigns of expansion and extraction on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s frontiers rather than struggles for survival. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>, first king of the Fourth Dynasty, held territory from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> in the west to the Sinai Peninsula in the east and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> in the south, and founded a settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buhen">Buhen</a> in Nubia that lasted two centuries. Under his successors there were military expeditions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and Nubia, and Egyptian influence reached up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> into what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. The Fifth Dynasty's inscriptions and reliefs refer to and depict campaigns in Nubia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> alongside its expeditions to the quarries and mines of Nubia and Sinai.</p><p>The Sixth Dynasty was the most aggressive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-i">Pepi I</a>'s reign was marked by aggressive expansion into Nubia, and at least five military expeditions were sent into Canaan; Merenre was active in Nubia too and sent officials to maintain Egyptian rule there from the northern border to the region south of the third cataract. These major military campaigns, with flourishing trade and mining, mark the dynasty's peak. The jubilee run, a royal rite established in the Old Kingdom, had the king run around markers that stood for the borders of Egypt, a display of the vigour that qualified him to go on ruling. The internal security that marked the whole period broke down only at its end, when the long reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii">Pepi II</a> gave way to succession struggles and the country slipped into civil wars within decades of his death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khafre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khafre.jpg" alt="Khafre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khafra_-_Quefr%C3%A9n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José-Manuel Benito, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During the Old Kingdom the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, not called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, became a living god who ruled absolutely and could demand the services and wealth of his subjects. He was the incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, and the stability of the cosmic cycles depended on him. The formerly independent states of the valley were reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a> under his rule, their old rulers made governors or tax collectors, and the royal residence remained at Memphis. The Fourth Dynasty (2613–2494 BCE) was the zenith of royal power: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>, then his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a> (2589–2566 BCE), whose sons Djedefre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a> may have quarrelled, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menkaure">Menkaure</a>, Shepseskaf and perhaps Djedefptah.</p><p>The Fifth Dynasty (2494–2345 BCE), from Userkaf, was marked by the growing importance of the cult of the sun god Ra, so that fewer efforts went to pyramid complexes and more to the sun temples of Abusir. Neferirkare Kakai (2475–2455 BCE) introduced the prenomen into the royal titulary, Nyuserre Ini (2445–2421 BCE) built extensively at Abusir and restarted royal activity at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, and Unas (2375–2345 BCE) was the earliest ruler to have the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> inscribed in his pyramid. Under the Sixth Dynasty (2345–2181 BCE) the king's power gradually weakened in favour of powerful nomarchs, the regional governors, who no longer belonged to the royal family; their charge became hereditary, creating local dynasties largely independent of the central authority.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Rulers and leaders in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre<span>26th century BCE</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu<span>26th century BCE</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/menkaure">Menkaure<span>26th century BCE</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu<span>26th century BCE</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza.jpg" alt="Great Pyramid of Giza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Pyramid_of_Giza_-_Pyramid_of_Khufu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Douwe C. van der Zee, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> attained its first sustained peak of civilisation in the Old Kingdom, the 'Age of the Pyramids', and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> looked back on it as a golden age, a grandiose height to which all future kingdoms aspired. Its monuments defined Egypt for the world: the Step Pyramid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>'s three pyramids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meidum">Meidum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a>, the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Pyramid of Khufu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, with which the fully developed pyramid style was reached, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>'s second pyramid and, in traditional thinking, the Great Sphinx of Giza, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menkaure">Menkaure</a>'s, the smallest of the three great pyramids. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> first inscribed under Unas are the earliest of their kind.</p><p>The Old Kingdom fixed the conventions of Egyptian art for the rest of its history. Frontality, composite composition and the hierarchy of scale, begun in the Early Dynastic Period and solidified now, made figures axial, symmetrical, proportional and reproducible, the head and legs in profile with the torso to the front, the king larger than all but the gods; male figures of the age have broad shoulders and long, muscled torsos, and the king was shown young and vital. Beyond the valley, the Old Kingdom's ships opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> and the sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>'s cedar, its settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buhen">Buhen</a> and its campaigns carried Egyptian rule deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, and its trade reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old Kingdom ended from within and from the sky. During the Sixth Dynasty the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> gradually weakened in favour of the nomarchs, whose offices became hereditary and whose local dynasties grew largely independent of Memphis. Internal disorders set in during the incredibly long reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii">Pepi II</a> (2278–2214 BCE) towards the dynasty's end; his death, certainly well after that of his intended heirs, may have set off succession struggles, and the country slipped into civil wars within decades of his reign's close.</p><p>The final blow was the drought of the 22nd century BCE, a drastic fall in rainfall that for at least some years between 2200 and 2150 BCE prevented the normal flooding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. Whatever its cause, the collapse was followed by decades of famine and strife, the period of disunity and relative cultural decline that Egyptologists call the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, beginning with the Seventh Dynasty; the tomb inscription of Ankhtifi, a nomarch of its early years, describes the pitiful state of the country when famine stalked the land. Some Egyptologists count the Seventh and Eighth Dynasties, still administered from Memphis, as the Old Kingdom's last phase. What survived was the memory of a golden age and its stone: the pyramids and their temples, the sculpture in granite and graywacke, the art whose conventions endured to the end of pharaonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Old Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> (2686–2181 BCE), ruled from Memphis by kings held to be living gods, raised the Step Pyramid and the pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, traded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> and campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, until hereditary nomarchs, the long reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii">Pepi II</a> and the drought of 2200–2150 BCE brought famine and collapse.</em></p>

